# Ask the owner

Stop and ask the owner. Wait for the answer. Do not pick, skip, or keep implementing.

Ask when any of these is true:

- More than one real choice exists (design, API, library, scope, naming that changes behavior).
- The decision is important (architecture, security, data, public API, phase scope).
- The work would cost many tokens (large read, broad search, many files, a long implementation).
- You would leave something out because it seems unimportant to build now.

An owner decision or an existing convention that already names the answer is not an open choice. Follow it.

This wins over rules that say to pick the cheapest or closest match on your own.

```text
❌ BAD — two cache backends fit; pick Redis and continue
✅ GOOD — "Memory or Redis for this phase? I'll wait."
```
